<section class="section wrap" id="lab"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="label">7 / INTERACTIVE EVALUATION LAB</p><h2>99% accurate.<br/>Still missing every violation.</h2></div><p>Explore the supplied 1,000-case example. “Violation” is the positive class. All values are synthetic, not company results.</p></div><div class="violation-visual"><div><span class="label">ZOOM IN / THE 10 ACTUAL VIOLATIONS</span><p id="violation-visual-summary">0 caught · 10 missed</p></div><div class="violation-markers" role="img" aria-label="All ten actual violations were missed" id="violation-markers">{Array.from({length:10},(_,i)=><span data-violation-dot={i} class="is-missed" aria-hidden="true">×</span>)}</div><div class="violation-key"><span><i class="key-caught"></i>Caught</span><span><i class="key-missed"></i>Missed</span><small>Each marker is one actual violation.</small></div></div><div class="lab-grid"><div class="lab-controls"><h3>Change the reviewer’s classifications</h3><p>Reference labels: <b>10 violations</b> and <b>990 allowed cases</b>.</p><label for="tp">Violations correctly caught <output id="tp-value">0 / 10</output></label><input type="range" id="tp" min="0" max="10" value="0"/><label for="fp">Allowed cases incorrectly flagged <output id="fp-value">0 / 990</output></label><input type="range" id="fp" min="0" max="990" value="0"/><div class="preset"><button id="all-allowed">Predict all allowed</button><button id="mixed">Try a mixed result</button></div><p class="small">These sliders change counts directly; they are not a model threshold or an empirical ROC curve.</p><a href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Metric definitions · Google ↗</a></div><div><table class="matrix"><caption>Confusion matrix · counts</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reference ↓ / prediction →</th><th scope="col">Violation</th><th scope="col">Allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Violation</th><td class="correct"><span>True positive</span><strong id="tp-cell">0</strong></td><td class="miss"><span>False negative</span><strong id="fn-cell">10</strong></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Allowed</th><td class="miss"><span>False positive</span><strong id="fp-cell">0</strong></td><td class="correct"><span>True negative</span><strong id="tn-cell">990</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="metrics"><div><span>Accuracy</span><strong id="accuracy">99.0%</strong><small>(TP + TN) / all cases</small></div><div><span>Precision</span><strong id="precision">N/A</strong><small>TP / (TP + FP)</small></div><div><span>Recall</span><strong id="recall">0.0%</strong><small>TP / (TP + FN)</small></div></div><p id="metric-story" class="insight" aria-live="polite">All 10 violations are missed. High accuracy hides complete failure to identify the positive class. Precision is undefined because nothing was flagged.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section wrap" id="calibration"><p class="label">8 / CALIBRATION WORKBENCH</p><h2>Disagreement is a signal.<br/>Investigate before assigning blame.</h2><div class="calibration-grid"><div><p>Illustrative labels from three reviewers. Each row is one item; the numbers below describe this tiny demonstration only.</p><table class="agreement"><caption>Synthetic review sample</caption><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>01</th><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td></tr><tr><th>02</th><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td></tr><tr class="split"><th>03</th><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td></tr><tr class="split"><th>04</th><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td><td><span class="judgment-allow">Allow</span></td><td><span class="judgment-flag">Flag</span></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="stat-row"><strong>50%<small>unanimous items · 2 / 4</small></strong><strong>66.7%<small>pairwise agreement · 8 / 12</small></strong></div><p class="small">Pairwise agreement counts the three reviewer pairs within each item. This is descriptive agreement, not chance-corrected agreement or accuracy.</p></div><div class="diagnostic"><h3>A proposed diagnostic sequence</h3><ol><li><b>Inspect the case.</b> Check missing context and the exact rule.</li><li><b>Compare rationales.</b> Look for recurring interpretation differences.</li><li><b>Check the version.</b> Ensure reviewers used the same criteria.</li><li><b>Adjudicate.</b> Ask the authorized owner to resolve the boundary.</li><li><b>Recalibrate.</b> Add contrasting examples and review a fresh sample.</li></ol><p>Consensus alone cannot prove correctness. Preserve dissent and uncertainty when the evidence does not support a settled answer.</p><a href={readings[4].url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source workflow · Label Studio ↗</a></div></div></section>
<HandshakeOperations />
<section class="section wrap" id="analysis"><p class="label">10 / ANALYTICAL PRACTICE</p><h2>Make the denominator explicit.</h2><div class="query-process" aria-label="How the analysis query works"><div><span>01 / JOIN</span><strong>Tasks + final reviews</strong><p>One row per evaluated item</p></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>02 / FILTER</span><strong>Resolved &amp; comparable</strong><p>Known labels, same policy version</p></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>03 / GROUP</span><strong>By policy category</strong><p>Count reviewed items and errors</p></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>04 / CALCULATE</span><strong>Errors ÷ reviewed items</strong><p>Report the category’s error rate</p></div></div><div class="two-grid"><div><p>This proposed query compares labels with a finalized reference label, grouped by policy category. The schema assumes one row per task and one final review per task.</p><p>Confirm uniqueness before joining. Exclude unresolved reviews deliberately, report how many were excluded, and avoid interpreting an unreviewed item as a correct one.</p><a href="https://sqlbolt.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Practice the SQL building blocks ↗</a></div><pre aria-label="Illustrative SQL query"><code>{`-- Illustrative schema, not production data.
SELECT t.policy_category,
       COUNT(*) AS reviewed_items,
       SUM(CASE WHEN t.label <> r.reference_label
                THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS errors,
       1.0 * SUM(CASE
         WHEN t.label <> r.reference_label
         THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*) AS error_rate
FROM tasks t
JOIN final_reviews r ON r.task_id = t.id
WHERE r.status = 'resolved'
  AND t.label IS NOT NULL
  AND r.reference_label IS NOT NULL
  AND t.policy_version = r.policy_version
GROUP BY t.policy_category;`}</code></pre></div></section>
<section class="section wrap" id="plan" aria-labelledby="preparation-title">
 <div class="section-head"><div><p class="label">11 / MY INTERVIEW PREPARATION</p><h2 id="preparation-title">From the invitation<br/>to the conversation.</h2></div><p>Since hearing from Handshake on October 19, I’ve been building this page to prepare for my October 26 interview for the Strategic Projects Associate, Safety role.</p></div>
 <div class="prep-dates" aria-label="Interview preparation timeline"><div><time datetime="2026-10-19">OCT 19</time><strong>The opportunity</strong><p>Begin connecting the role to my experience.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><time datetime="2026-10-22">OCT 22</time><strong>Preparation in progress</strong><p>Bring the research, role mapping and questions together.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="prep-interview"><time datetime="2026-10-26">OCT 26</time><strong>The interview</strong><p>An opportunity to discuss my thinking and learn from the team.</p><small>Upcoming</small></div></div>
 <p class="prep-introduction">I wanted to go into the conversation with a clear understanding of the work. This page brings together my preparation so far, organized around the questions I want to discuss.</p>
 <ol class="prep-work">
  <li><span>01</span><div><h3>Understand the business behind the role.</h3><p>I brought together public materials on Handshake AI’s contributor model and the role of human data in model development. I organized the ideas into a workflow so I could connect contributor expertise, project guidance and useful outputs.</p><a href="#handshake-business">My understanding of Handshake ↗</a></div></li>
  <li><span>02</span><div><h3>Connect the job description to concrete work.</h3><p>I broke the responsibilities into tasks, stakeholders and outputs, then mapped them to my governance, approval-workflow and research experience. I also identified responsibilities where I would need training or supervised practice.</p><a href="#role-fit">Responsibilities and my experience ↗</a></div></li>
  <li><span>03</span><div><h3>Build a reference library for the questions I’m exploring.</h3><p>I assembled source-based notes on policy interpretation, human review, evaluation and delivery. Each note connects a concept to an example and a possible application, giving me a place to revisit the original source and examine the conditions behind a rule.</p><a href="#readings">My reading notes ↗</a></div></li>
  <li><span>04</span><div><h3>Make the operational challenges tangible.</h3><p>I developed illustrative workflows for reviewer disagreement, unclear guidance, data problems and delivery pressure. I structured each response around what to investigate, who to involve and what evidence would show that the issue was resolved.</p><a href="#operations">My operating approach ↗</a></div></li>
  <li><span>05</span><div><h3>Explain my contribution honestly.</h3><p>I shaped a personal narrative around what I have done, how I learn and why this role interests me. My goal for October 26 is to explain that connection clearly, ask thoughtful questions and understand where the team would most need my contribution.</p><a href="#personal-statement">My personal note ↗</a></div></li>
 </ol>
 <div class="prep-closing"><span class="label">WHAT I WANT TO BRING TO OCTOBER 26</span><p>A considered understanding of the role, concrete examples from my experience and an openness to learning how the team actually works.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="section wrap personal-statement" id="personal-statement" aria-labelledby="personal-statement-title">
 <div class="personal-note"><HandshakeArt variant="note" />
  <header><p class="label">12 / A PERSONAL NOTE</p><h2 id="personal-statement-title">Curious about the work.<br/>Ready to grow into it.</h2><p class="personal-lead">I’m Min, an AI Governance Analyst at Krafton and a graduating senior at UIUC. As I think about where to begin the next chapter of my career, I’m looking for work I can care about deeply, people I can learn from and the chance to become someone a team can count on.</p></header>
  <div class="personal-copy personal-narrative personal-journey">
   <section class="personal-chapter"><div class="personal-chapter-label"><span class="personal-chapter-dot" aria-hidden="true">01</span><span>Curiosity</span><h3>Look beyond the result.</h3></div><p>Through my work in AI governance and research, I’ve found myself drawn to the questions behind a result. Why were these requests classified differently? What information did the reviewer need? Was the problem in the case itself, or in the guidance we gave people? Working through approval requests, automation cases and model evaluations has helped me understand what I enjoy: <mark>taking something unclear, looking closely at the evidence</mark> and making it easier for someone else to decide what to do next.</p></section>
   <section class="personal-chapter"><div class="personal-chapter-label"><span class="personal-chapter-dot" aria-hidden="true">02</span><span>Purpose</span><h3>Make judgment useful.</h3></div><p>That is what draws me to this role at Handshake. A policy still needs to become <mark>instructions that people can use</mark>, judgments they can explain and work a customer can rely on. I want to be involved in those details. My experience gives me a starting point in analysis, review workflows and stakeholder communication, and I’m excited to develop that foundation in safety operations.</p></section>
   <section class="personal-chapter"><div class="personal-chapter-label"><span class="personal-chapter-dot" aria-hidden="true">03</span><span>Learning</span><h3>Stay open to feedback.</h3></div><p>I’m still early in my career, and there is a lot I want to learn. My approach is to prepare, ask specific questions, put what I learn into practice and seek feedback while I can still act on it. Building this page is part of that process. I wanted to understand Handshake’s business, think through the day-to-day responsibilities and be honest with myself about both what I can contribute and what I need to learn. On the team, I would bring that same effort to real cases and <mark>use feedback to make the next piece of work better</mark>.</p></section>
   <section class="personal-chapter"><div class="personal-chapter-label"><span class="personal-chapter-dot" aria-hidden="true">04</span><span>Ownership</span><h3>Follow through.</h3></div><p>I would start by learning the team’s standards and taking responsibility for a clear scope of work. I can contribute by organizing evidence, investigating patterns, improving guidance and communicating what needs a decision. Over time, I want to <mark>earn more ownership by following through</mark>, raising questions early and showing that I can make thoughtful decisions as the work becomes more complex.</p></section>
   <section class="personal-chapter"><div class="personal-chapter-label"><span class="personal-chapter-dot" aria-hidden="true">05</span><span>Direction</span><h3>Grow through the work.</h3></div><p>What makes this opportunity meaningful to me is the chance to connect my curiosity about AI with useful, everyday work. I want to learn from people who take both quality and execution seriously, and contribute to the work alongside them. As I finish university, that is the kind of growth I’m looking for: becoming more capable through practice, <mark>staying open to correction</mark> and making a contribution that the people around me can see and trust.</p></section>
  </div>
  <div class="personal-growth-visual"><p class="label">HOW I WANT TO KEEP GROWING</p><ol aria-label="My approach to learning">
   <li><span class="growth-symbol" aria-hidden="true">?</span><div><h3>Ask thoughtfully</h3><p>Understand the decision.</p></div></li>
   <li><span class="growth-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↗</span><div><h3>Put it into practice</h3><p>Make the next step useful.</p></div></li>
   <li><span class="growth-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↔</span><div><h3>Seek feedback</h3><p>Listen, adjust and try again.</p></div></li>
   <li><span class="growth-symbol" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><div><h3>Earn ownership</h3><p>Build trust by following through.</p></div></li>
  </ol></div>
  <footer><p>I would be excited to bring my curiosity, care and willingness to learn to Handshake, and earn the team’s trust one piece of work at a time.</p><span>Min Kim</span></footer>
 </div>
</section>
</main>
<script>
 import '../../scripts/handshake-motion';
 import '../../scripts/handshake-art-motion';
 const sectionMenu = document.getElementById('study-side-menu')!;
 sectionMenu.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(link => link.addEventListener('click', () => sectionMenu.hidePopover()));
 const tp=document.getElementById('tp') as HTMLInputElement,fp=document.getElementById('fp') as HTMLInputElement;
 const put=(id:string,text:string)=>{document.getElementById(id)!.textContent=text;};
 function update(){const t=Number(tp.value),f=Number(fp.value),fn=10-t,tn=990-f;put('violation-visual-summary',`${t} caught · ${fn} missed`);document.getElementById('violation-markers')!.setAttribute('aria-label',`${t} of ten actual violations caught; ${fn} missed`);document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-violation-dot]').forEach((dot,i)=>{const caught=i<t;dot.className=caught?'is-caught':'is-missed';dot.textContent=caught?'✓':'×';});put('tp-value',`${t} / 10`);put('fp-value',`${f} / 990`);put('tp-cell',String(t));put('fp-cell',String(f));put('fn-cell',String(fn));put('tn-cell',String(tn));put('accuracy',((t+tn)/10).toFixed(1)+'%');put('precision',t+f?((t/(t+f))*100).toFixed(1)+'%':'N/A');put('recall',(t*10).toFixed(1)+'%');put('metric-story',t===0&&f===0?'All 10 violations are missed. High accuracy hides complete failure to identify the positive class. Precision is undefined because nothing was flagged.':`${t} of 10 violations caught; ${fn} missed. ${f} allowed cases incorrectly flagged. Review severity and category-level results before drawing a quality conclusion.`);}
 tp.addEventListener('input',update);fp.addEventListener('input',update);document.getElementById('all-allowed')!.addEventListener('click',()=>{tp.value='0';fp.value='0';update();});document.getElementById('mixed')!.addEventListener('click',()=>{tp.value='8';fp.value='20';update();});
</script>
</body></html>
